---

<DocShell
   title="Run an audit"
   summary="<p><code>a1 audit</code> runs a WCAG audit of a site, a page, a feature, or a mobile app, with AI agents doing the testing. It splits the work into small units, lets several agents test in parallel, saves every result as it arrives, and writes a report you can hand to a client.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section>
      <h2 id="start">Start an audit</h2>
      <p>
         The quickest way is to ask an agent that has the
         <a href="/guides/agent-skill"><code>a11ied-audit</code> skill</a>.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Audit https://example.com against WCAG 2.2 AA with a11ied. Do not submit the contact form. Use four workers.</code></pre>
      <p>The agent runs these commands. You can run them yourself.</p>
      <pre><code>a1 audit init https://example.com --rule "Do not submit the contact form"
a1 audit discover
a1 audit scan
a1 audit status</code></pre>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>init</code></dt>
         <dd>Creates the audit. <code>--rule</code> states something workers must not do, and every unit of work repeats it. <code>--access</code> says how to reach the target, such as where the test account is.</dd>
         <dt><code>discover</code></dt>
         <dd>Finds the site's pages and adds them.</dd>
         <dt><code>scan</code></dt>
         <dd>Runs the automated checks on every page.</dd>
         <dt><code>status</code></dt>
         <dd>Shows how much is done.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         An audit lives in <code>.a11ied/audits/&lt;id&gt;/</code>: a database, a folder of
         screenshots, and the report. It survives a crashed agent, a closed terminal, and a
         restart.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="how">How the work is divided</h2>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>Page</dt>
         <dd>One thing to test: a page, a screen, a state of one such as an open dialog, or a flow such as checkout.</dd>
         <dt>Test pass</dt>
         <dd>The criteria tested with one kind of interaction: <code>semantics</code>, <code>keyboard</code>, <code>visual</code>, <code>media</code>, <code>forms</code>, <code>pointer</code>, and <code>consistency</code> across pages.</dd>
         <dt>Unit</dt>
         <dd>One pass on one page, such as <code>P3.keyboard</code>. A worker claims a unit, tests it, and submits an outcome for each of its criteria.</dd>
         <dt>Finding</dt>
         <dd>One defect. The same defect on ten pages is one finding with ten occurrences.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>A page is loaded and exercised once per pass, not once per criterion.</p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="scope">Choose the scope</h2>
      <p>The default is WCAG 2.2 Level AA: 55 criteria.</p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>A whole site</dt>
         <dd><code>a1 audit init &lt;url&gt;</code>, then <code>a1 audit discover</code>. Remove pages with <code>a1 audit page exclude &lt;id&gt; --reason "..."</code>. On a large site, keep one page per template plus the pages in key tasks.</dd>
         <dt>One page</dt>
         <dd><code>a1 audit init &lt;url&gt; --page</code></dd>
         <dt>A state</dt>
         <dd><code>a1 audit page add &lt;url&gt; --kind state --name "Menu open" --notes "Activate the Menu button"</code></dd>
         <dt>A flow</dt>
         <dd><code>--kind flow</code>, with the steps in <code>--notes</code>.</dd>
         <dt>Chosen criteria</dt>
         <dd><code>--criteria 1.4.3,2.4.7</code></dd>
         <dt>One kind of testing</dt>
         <dd><code>--passes keyboard,forms</code></dd>
         <dt>Another level or version</dt>
         <dd><code>--level A</code>, <code>--level AAA</code>, <code>--wcag 2.1</code></dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         Criteria that compare pages, such as 3.2.3 Consistent Navigation, need at least two
         pages. A one-page audit reports them as not evaluated.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="split">What is automated</h2>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>Automated</dt>
         <dd>Finding pages. axe failures, which become findings. Measurements: tab order, focus styles, overflow at 320 pixels wide and at 200 percent zoom, target sizes, and lists of media and forms. Claims, progress, and the report.</dd>
         <dt>Judged by a worker</dt>
         <dd>Every pass and every "not applicable". Whether an automated finding is real. Everything a scanner cannot see, such as whether alt text describes the image or an error message helps. Severity and the fix.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         The scan never passes a criterion. Its measurements reach workers as facts to check.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="workers">Run the workers</h2>
      <p>
         A worker is an agent in a loop: claim a unit, test it, submit, repeat. Start several
         with different names and they take different units.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 audit next --worker w1
a1 audit submit P1.keyboard --worker w1 --file result.json</code></pre>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>next</code></dt>
         <dd>Prints the work packet: the page, the rules, the procedure, each criterion with when it fails, the scan's measurements, findings already recorded, and a template for the submission.</dd>
         <dt><code>submit</code></dt>
         <dd>Takes one JSON document and accepts or rejects it whole. A rejection lists every problem, such as a criterion with no outcome. For a finding on a web page, the worker gives a selector and <code>submit</code> takes the screenshot.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         VoiceOver, NVDA, and a physical device can each serve one worker at a time. When the
         audit uses one, give one worker <code>--pass semantics</code> and the others
         <code>--not-pass semantics</code>. The simulated screen reader runs in parallel.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="track">Watch progress</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 audit status
a1 audit dashboard
a1 audit findings
a1 audit log --unit P3.forms</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>dashboard</code> serves a live page on your machine: pages by test pass, which
         worker holds what, what is blocked and why, the findings so far, and a link to the
         report in progress.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="stuck">When something is stuck</h2>
      <dl class="defs defs--wide">
         <dt>A worker died</dt>
         <dd>Nothing is lost. Its claim ends after 20 minutes without activity and the unit returns to the queue. A worker that returns under the same name gets its unit back.</dd>
         <dt>A unit cannot be tested</dt>
         <dd>The worker blocks it with a reason, and work on other units continues. Fix the cause, then <code>a1 audit reopen &lt;unit&gt; --reason "..."</code>.</dd>
         <dt>One criterion cannot be decided</dt>
         <dd>The worker records it as <code>cannot-tell</code> with the reason. The report lists it under Not verified.</dd>
         <dt>A rule forbids part of a test</dt>
         <dd>A rule against sending a form still lets workers test its validation. Only what lies past the forbidden step, such as the success message, is <code>cannot-tell</code>.</dd>
         <dt>A state could not be reached</dt>
         <dd>The scan lists the controls that open a menu, dialog, or tab, and every submission accounts for each. One a worker could not reach is recorded as not tested.</dd>
         <dt>A tool or device is missing</dt>
         <dd>Record it for the whole audit with <code>a1 audit limitation "..." --criterion 4.1.2</code>. The report lists it under Not verified.</dd>
         <dt>A unit keeps failing</dt>
         <dd>After three abandoned attempts it blocks itself. Read <code>a1 audit log --unit &lt;id&gt;</code>.</dd>
         <dt>A result looks wrong</dt>
         <dd><code>a1 audit reopen &lt;unit&gt;</code> queues it again. <code>a1 audit finding dismiss</code>, <code>merge</code>, and <code>update</code> correct findings.</dd>
      </dl>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="report">The report</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 audit report</code></pre>
      <p>
         This writes <code>audit-report.html</code>, <code>.md</code>, and <code>.json</code>
         to the audit's <code>report/</code> folder. The HTML file is self-contained, with
         screenshots inside it. Print it from a browser for a PDF.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>Summary</dt>
         <dd>The result in one statement, findings by severity, and criteria by outcome.</dd>
         <dt>What to fix first</dt>
         <dd>Findings ordered by severity, then by how many pages they affect.</dd>
         <dt>Findings</dt>
         <dd>For each: the problem, who it affects, the fix, where it occurs, and a screenshot of the element with an outline around it.</dd>
         <dt>Results by criterion</dt>
         <dd>Fails, Passes, Not applicable, Not verified, or Not evaluated, for every criterion in scope.</dd>
         <dt>Not verified</dt>
         <dd>What could not be established, and why.</dd>
         <dt>Scope and method</dt>
         <dd>The pages and states tested, what was excluded, and how the testing was done.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         A report written before every unit is done is marked interim. A report never states
         conformance while a criterion is failing, unfinished, or not verified.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="mobile">Mobile apps</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 audit init "Acme iOS 4.2" --platform ios --access "Simulator: iPhone 16"
a1 audit page add "Tab bar > Search" --name "Search"</code></pre>
      <p>
         Tracking, workers, findings, and the report work the same way. There is no automated
         scan, and a1 does not drive the app. Workers operate it with the platform's own
         tools, such as <code>xcrun simctl</code> or <code>adb</code>. Give each worker its
         own simulator or device.
      </p>
      <p>
         On Android, <a href="/reference/cli#talkback"><code>a1 talkback</code></a> prints
         what TalkBack says at each stop.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
